  "narrative": "All returned estimates and intervals are signed to each claim's registered direction, so positive signed evidence supports the registered hypothesis. For the continuous dependency outcomes, I used the run's approximate 10% population-scale benchmarks while also checking absolute clinical magnitude and cell precision. PTEN loss showed clinically meaningful associations with stronger dependency_STAT3 in two additional contexts: low serum (H182, signed estimate +0.239, lower +0.163) and high mutation burden (H183, signed estimate +0.285, lower +0.188). These are accepted as dataset-specific context associations, not causal claims, and the effects are far above the STAT3 benchmark. TP53 loss showed robust signed evidence for the accepted weaker dependency_USP7 direction in high-quality inflammatory and EMT screens: H184 signed estimate +0.468 with lower +0.286, and H185 signed estimate +0.329 with lower +0.153. Both are well above the USP7 benchmark and are accepted. SMARCA4 loss continued to show very large signed associations with stronger dependency_SMARCA2 in high-quality IFN-high (H186, lower +0.705) and EMT-high screens (H187, lower +0.706); because the main effect is already very large, these tests function as robustness bounds and are accepted. MSI-high showed a very large signed association with stronger dependency_WRN in IFN-high lines (H191, signed estimate +0.825, lower +0.781), well above the WRN benchmark, and is accepted. CDKN2A loss in TP53-wildtype lines under high Cas9 showed a clinically meaningful signed association with stronger dependency_MDM2 (H194, signed estimate +0.141, lower +0.077), supporting the new pathway-relevant association under a screen-quality constraint. Notably, H196 tests CDKN2A loss within TP53-loss lines and also found a clinically meaningful positive signed effect (estimate +0.111, lower +0.035), which does not attenuate relative to the TP53-wildtype estimate in H192. Thus I accept H196 as evidence of a dataset-specific CDKN2A/MDM2 association in TP53-loss lines, but this weakens a simple p14ARF-MDM2-p53-context-only explanation and leaves open that the association may reflect correlated CDKN2A genomic features or other biology. H197 tested whether CDKN2A loss in TP53-wildtype lines also associates with weaker dependency_USP7; the signed estimate is essentially null and negative (-0.003), with a 95% interval from -0.025 to +0.020. The interval does not support the registered positive USP7 association and, given the wrong point estimate and the multiple-testing context, I reject the positive association while noting that a very small positive effect cannot be excluded. H198 tested adenocarcinoma-specific heterogeneity of the large SMARCA4/SMARCA2 association; the interaction is near null and signed negative (-0.028), with upper bound +0.020, below the SMARCA2 clinical-scale benchmark. This does not support the registered direction of stronger adenocarcinoma-specific enhancement, so I reject it. The lower bound could permit a modest opposite interaction, but that is not the registered claim and is not currently worth prioritization. H201 tested BRCA2 loss as a predictor of stronger dependency_DHX9; the signed estimate is positive but small (+0.012) and crosses null, with an upper bound that barely reaches the DHX9 benchmark. I leave it unresolved and deferred rather than accepting or closing, because the interval does not exclude a clinically meaningful positive effect, but the current evidence is weak and multiplicity is large. Voluntary validation budget is exhausted, so no validation request is made. All conclusions remain observational associations within the supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 14 appraise update. Validation budget exhausted; no new validation possible. New accepted context/robustness associations: PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency in low serum and high mutation burden; TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency in high-quality IFN-high and EMT-high screens; SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency in high-quality IFN-high and EMT-high screens; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency in IFN-high lines; CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency in TP53-wildtype high-Cas9 screens and also in TP53-loss screens. The latter suggests the CDKN2A/MDM2 association is not strictly p53-context-dependent in this dataset and may be confounded by correlated genomic features. Null or unsupported: CDKN2A loss in TP53-wildtype lines did not support an extension to USP7; adenocarcinoma interaction did not support SMARCA4/SMARCA2 subgroup enhancement; BRCA2 loss for DHX9 remains unresolved near null. Current supported core associations remain SMARCA4/SMARCA2, KEAP1/NFE2L2, TP53/USP7, MSI-high/WRN, PTEN/STAT3, and CDKN2A/MDM2. Active unevaluated claims include H189, H195, H199, H200, H202-H206 and the new CDKN2A/MDM2 interaction by TP53 status. Next analysis priorities: prioritize active unevaluated context tests H189, H195, H199-H206, plus the new CDKN2A/MDM2 interaction if slots remain. Use signed estimates and benchmarks: STAT3 ~0.028, USP7 ~0.018, SMARCA2 ~0.042, NFE2L2 ~0.009, WRN ~0.030, MDM2 ~0.027, DHX9/KIF18A and other near-null outcomes ~0.03. Interpret interactions relative to very large main effects as bounds; small interactions are not failures unless interval excludes meaningful heterogeneity. All inference remains dataset-specific association, not causality, therapeutic dependency, or generalizability."
}

