tative accepted claims include H9, H25, H33, H42, H54, H57, H94, H153, H162, H190, and H191.\n5. PTEN loss is associated with stronger dependency_STAT3. This is a broad and repeatedly supported association, with validation in several contexts including high-quality screens, PTEN/TP53 contexts, high-IFN, high-EMT, and related boundaries using R66, R106, R107, R120, and R133. Representative accepted claims include H80, H84, H85, H86, H88, H98, H99, H109, H110, H113, H114, H115, H117, H122, H124, H125, H132, H133, H150, H154, H155, H156, H166, H167, H168, H170, H171, H172, H174, H180, H182, and H183.\n6. CDKN2A loss is newly supported as associated with stronger dependency_MDM2 in this dataset. It is accepted in TP53-wildtype lines, high-screen-quality TP53-wildtype lines, high-Cas9 TP53-wildtype lines, TP53-loss lines, MTAP-intact lines, and other locus-specificity checks, with signed estimates clinically meaningful and lower bounds above the MDM2 benchmark near 0.027. Representative accepted claims include H192, H194, H195, H196, and H209. The persistence in TP53-loss and MTAP-intact contexts makes a simple p14ARF-MDM2-p53 or MTAP co-deletion explanation insufficient in this dataset, although mechanisms remain unproven.\n\nNegative and unsupported findings.\nDirect dependency effects for matched receptor or MAPK alterations are generally not supported. BRAF V600E did not support stronger dependency_BRAF. EGFR amplification did not yield a stable, validated clinically meaningful effect on dependency_EGFR. ERBB2 amplification was near-null for dependency_ERBB2 and for the tested STAT3 contrasts. KRAS mutation was not a robust predictor of dependency_KRAS or dependency_RIT1. These null or non-decisive results are not evidence against underlying biology, but this dataset does not support measured genomic alteration self-dependency as a stable predictor.\nCanonical TP53 loss did not robustly predict stronger dependency_MDM2; QC-restricted and validated checks support rejection or non-decisive small effects. MYC amplification did not support dependency_DHX9, dependency_RPL11, dependency_POLR2A, dependency_KIF18A, or dependency_MDM2 in TP53-wildtype contexts. MSI-high did not support dependency_POLR2A after validation. PTEN loss did not robustly modify dependency_KRAS among KRAS-mutant lines. CDKN2A loss did not support the tested USP7 direction. The constructed markers D/E/F are uninformative for dependency_STAT3 and should not be interpreted as biological markers. Representative negative or unsupported claims include H1, H2, H3, H4, H7, H10, H11, H12, H13, H14, H16, H63, H64, H66, H67, H68, H79, H81, H82, H89, H100, H101, H102, H103, H107, H108, H138, H139, H140, H143, H146, H158, H159, H160, H197, H200, and H206.\nInteractions require conservative interpretation. For the very large main effects on SMARCA2, NFE2L2, WRN, and MDM2, many interaction tests are near null, indicating no strong evidence for the tested modifiers rather than proof of homogeneity. This includes H76, H77, H90, H97, H119, H131, H134, H137, H141, H142, H149, H151, H152, H193, H198, H202, H203, and H207. Some interaction intervals are too wide to rule out clinically meaningful heterogeneity.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty.\nH201 remains unresolved and now deferred for BRCA2 loss and dependency_DHX9: the small positive signed estimate is compatible with a real effect but is not clinically established. Active claims H210 through H215 test high-ploidy CDKN2A/MDM2, MSI-high or BRCA2 high-TMB DHX9 hypotheses, and low-Cas9 robustness for USP7, SMARCA2, and NFE2L2. H216 is exploratory because it reverses H206: RB1-loss lines showed weaker rather than stronger dependency_DHX9, the raw opposite of the originally registered direction. RB1/DHX9 remains hypothesis-generating and could be confounded by MYC amplification, ploidy, cell-cycle state, or multiple testing. No external validation is available for H216 or the CDKN2A/MDM2 association because the validation budget is exhausted.\n\nMost informative next analysis.\nThe active low-Cas9-activity panel H213 through H215 is the most important technical follow-up. If accepted associations survive lower Cas9 activity, they are less likely to depend on highly potent screens; if they attenuate sharply, screen potency becomes an explicit context boundary. The DHX9 active tests H211 and H212 are the most important new biology tests for replication-stress predictors beyond WRN. H210 and the newly registered low-ploidy CDKN2A and STAT3 boundary tests help bound accepted high-ploidy associations. The newly registered MYC-negative RB1/DHX9 specificity test is secondary and exploratory. All next analyses should remain discovery-only and should require interval-scale evidence before accepting new context-specific claims.",
