  "narrative": "No validation response deadlines are listed for this synthesis, the voluntary validation budget is exhausted, and there are no due assessments. I therefore make no new status changes. Interpreting all returned estimates as already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, and using about 10 percent of the absolute outcome mean as the practical clinical-significance benchmark, the final supported dataset-specific associations are: SMARCA4 loss is strongly associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency; MSI-high is associated with stronger WRN dependency; PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency; and CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. These effects are large or consistently clinically meaningful, internally replicated across many genomic, technical, lineage, expression, proliferation, and screen-quality strata, and several are independently validated. Negative findings include canonical self-dependency expectations for BRAF V600E, ERBB2 amplification, and KRAS mutation; null or unsupported MYC-amplification effects on DHX9, RPL11, POLR2A, and KIF18A; unsupported PIK3CA-mutation receptor-bypass hypotheses for EGFR and ERBB2; lack of robust TP53-loss prediction of MDM2 dependency in the tests performed; and failure to establish library-context modifiers for SMARCA2 or USP7. Unresolved uncertainty remains concentrated on EGFR amplification for dependency_EGFR, small and imprecise KRAS-mutation and NF1-loss contrasts, and weak exploratory DHX9/RB1 signals. Interaction tests generally fail to establish modifiers and should not be read as proof of homogeneity. These conclusions describe associations in the supplied lung-lineage dataset; they do not establish causality, therapeutic targeting, or external generalizability. The most informative next analysis is external replication of the six accepted associations and well-powered testing of EGFR amplification and KRAS/NF1 contrasts; additional internal subgroup slicing is now low value relative to multiplicity and power limitations.",
  "research_notes": "Final synthesis after iteration 20. No due validation assessments and no validation budget remains. Persistent accepted findings are dataset-specific associations, not causal claims: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2; TP53 loss with weaker USP7; MSI-high with stronger WRN; PTEN loss with stronger STAT3; CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2. Negative findings include canonical receptor self-dependency hypotheses, MYC-amplification targets tested, PIK3CA receptor-bypass hypotheses, robust TP53-loss MDM2 predictions, and demonstrated library modifiers. Unresolved candidates are EGFR amplification, KRAS/NF1 small effects, and DHX9/RB1 weak signals. Next priority is external replication and well-powered EGFR/KRAS-NF1 testing, not further internal subgroup slicing."
}

