  "narrative": "No assessments are due, so I do not change any existing claim status. I register eight new proposals: four targeted refinements of the remaining small-effect candidates, one new co-deletion biology test, and three exploratory effect-modification tests for established robust associations. All returned estimates and intervals will be interpreted as already signed to the registered direction. For binary dependency hypotheses, direction -1 claims that the exposed group has a more negative, i.e. stronger, dependency score; direction +1 claims a less negative, i.e. weaker, dependency score. For interaction claims, a positive signed value supports a stronger version of the registered main direction in the named subgroup relative with the complement.\n\nThe four refinements are intended to reduce confounding by background genotype, screen technicality, lineage, and expression context rather than add broad subgroup slicing. EGFR amplification versus wild type is tested for stronger dependency_EGFR among KRAS-wild-type, higher-screen-quality adenocarcinoma lines. If EGFR-amplified lung adenocarcinoma cells are genuinely lineage and pathway coherent, they should show stronger EGFR dependency only when co-mutations that may bypass receptor dependence are absent. NF1 loss versus intact is tested for weaker dependency_KRAS among KRAS-wild-type, higher-quality, higher-Cas9 adenocarcinoma lines. NF1 loss can attenuate RAS activation, so NF1-loss cells that still depend on KRAS may have less negative KRAS scores; a positive signed effect would support this bypass-like biology. KRAS mutation versus wild type is tested in the same high-quality/high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma context for stronger KRAS dependency, addressing whether weak opposite-signed high-Cas9 signals persist when lineage is held constant. RB1 loss versus intact is tested for weaker dependency_DHX9 among MYC-low, higher-quality, higher-Cas9 adenocarcinoma lines; the prior signal was small and context-dependent, and MYC-low restriction avoids conflating DHX9 with MYC transcriptional stress biology.\n\nThe MTAP proposal tests whether the accepted CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 association is modified by MTAP co-deletion among CDKN2A-loss lines. MTAP and CDKN2A are frequently co-deleted at 9p21. If MTAP co-loss marks a distinct vulnerability, dependency_MDM2 could be stronger in MTAP-loss than MTAP-intact CDKN2A-loss lines; alternatively, MTAP status could be irrelevant. The registered direction is -1 for stronger MDM2 dependency, but the anticipated raw contrast is uncertain.\n\nThe three interaction proposals test whether strong associations are stable across proliferation, interferon signaling, and serum contexts. Proliferation rate can influence essentiality because rapidly dividing cells have different DNA repair, ribosome biogenesis, and p53-pathway demands. Interferon signaling may alter tumor-cell phenotypes and chromatin dependencies. Serum level can alter growth-factor and STAT signaling. These are not causal modifiers; they are dataset-specific heterogeneity checks. Because independent validation budget is exhausted, any new acceptances would remain descriptive associations in selected subsets, not causal or externally generalizable claims. I will assess new results against both statistical uncertainty and the approximate clinical-significance benchmark of about a 10% relative shift in outcome magnitude.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 21 update. No due assessments and no new validation requests remain. Persistent accepted dataset-specific associations are SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2, TP53 loss with weaker USP7, MSI-high with stronger WRN, PTEN loss with stronger STAT3, and CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2. Negative/unsupported findings include canonical receptor self-dependency hypotheses for EGFR, ERBB2, BRAF, KRAS mutation, MYC-amplification targets, PIK3CA receptor bypass, TP53-loss MDM2 as a robust effect, and tested library modifiers. New registered proposals focus on remaining unresolved small-effect candidates and one mechanism-relevant co-deletion: EGFR amplification in KRAS-wt adenocarcinoma high-quality; NF1 loss in KRAS-wt adenocarcinoma high-quality/high-Cas9; KRAS mutation self-dependency in high-quality/high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma; RB1/DHX9 in MYC-low high-quality/high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma; MTAP loss within CDKN2A-loss for MDM2; and interaction/heterogeneity checks for TP53/USP7 by proliferation, SMARCA4/SMARCA2 by interferon signature, and PTEN/STAT3 by serum. These are final-stage internal refinements, not causal or generalizability tests. If new results are accepted, they should be labeled dataset-specific associations in selected strata."
}

