  "narrative": "All due results are interpreted using the already-signed convention. For direction -1, a positive signed mean difference indicates stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1, it indicates weaker dependency. For interactions, a positive signed value supports the registered main-direction effect being stronger in the named subgroup than in the complement. I used approximately 10% of the absolute population mean dependency score as the practical benchmark: roughly 0.041 for SMARCA2, 0.018 for USP7, 0.028 for STAT3, 0.030 for WRN, 0.027 for MDM2, 0.025 for EGFR/ERBB2/DHX9, and smaller for NFE2L2 because its mean is closer to zero.\n\nH267 is accepted. PTEN loss versus intact PTEN, restricted to lines without TP53, KEAP1, MSI-high, and SMARCA4 alterations, shows a positive signed difference of +0.295 (95% Welch interval 0.218 to 0.372) for dependency_STAT3 with direction -1. This indicates substantially stronger STAT3 dependency among PTEN-loss lines in a relatively clean genomic background. The effect is far above the STAT3 clinical benchmark and well separated from zero. The cell sizes are adequate (122 exposed, 839 comparator). Acceptance is limited to this dataset and these restrictions; it is not causal evidence.\n\nH268 is accepted. TP53 loss versus intact TP53, excluding PTEN, KEAP1, MSI-high, and SMARCA4 alterations, gives +0.409 (0.310 to 0.509) for dependency_USP7 with direction +1. This indicates weaker USP7 dependency among TP53-loss lines in a clean background. It is much larger than the USP7 benchmark and internally consistent with the previously accepted TP53-loss/USP7 associations. The effect remains dataset-specific.\n\nH269 is accepted. KEAP1 loss versus intact KEAP1, excluding TP53, PTEN, MSI-high, and SMARCA4 alterations, gives +0.803 (0.774 to 0.832) for dependency_NFE2L2 with direction +1. This indicates very much weaker NFE2L2 dependency among KEAP1-loss lines in the clean background. This is a large, stable dataset-specific association, not a causal claim.\n\nH270 is accepted. CDKN2A loss versus intact CDKN2A, excluding TP53, KEAP1, MSI-high, and SMARCA4 alterations, gives +0.141 (0.090 to 0.192) for dependency_MDM2 with direction -1. This indicates stronger MDM2 dependency among CDKN2A-loss lines. The point estimate exceeds the MDM2 benchmark and the interval excludes zero. This supports internal specificity of the CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 association beyond co-occurring TP53 or other major alterations, but only within this dataset.\n\nH271 is accepted. MSI-high versus microsatellite-stable, excluding TP53, KEAP1, SMARCA4, and PTEN loss, gives +0.774 (0.728 to 0.821) for dependency_WRN with direction -1. This indicates markedly stronger WRN dependency among MSI-high lines even in a genomic background excluding other strong predictors. The exposed cell is smaller (55) but meets the minimum and the interval is tight enough to support a very large association.\n\nH272 is unresolved and closed. ERBB2 amplification versus no ERBB2 amplification in EGFR-amplification-negative, higher-quality screens has a signed estimate of -0.0032 (-0.0399 to 0.0336) for dependency_EGFR with direction +1. The point estimate is essentially null and slightly opposite the registered direction. The interval includes clinically meaningful positive effects near the upper edge, but the evidence does not support the proposed RTK-crosstalk claim. No further internal follow-up is warranted given multiplicity and limited value.\n\nH273 is unresolved and closed. EGFR amplification versus no EGFR amplification in ERBB2-amplification-negative, higher-quality screens gives +0.0312 (-0.0054 to 0.0679) for dependency_ERBB2 with direction +1. The point estimate suggests weaker ERBB2 dependency with EGFR amplification and is near the benchmark, but the interval includes zero and is wide. This is not enough to accept a receptor-crosstalk association, and further slicing would add multiplicity risk.\n\nH274 is unresolved and closed. MSI-high versus microsatellite-stable among high mutation-burden lines gives +0.0216 (-0.0160 to 0.0592) for dependency_DHX9 with direction -1. The estimate is below the DHX9 benchmark and uncertain. The mechanistic R-loop/replication-stress idea remains plausible but unsupported as a clinically meaningful dataset-specific association.\n\nH275 is unresolved and closed. The interaction of PTEN loss and high ASCL2 expression on dependency_STAT3 gives +0.0121 (-0.1033 to 0.1276). The estimate is small and the interval is very wide. It neither accepts nor excludes a clinically meaningful modifier effect. Given multiplicity and no validation budget, this is closed as insufficiently supported.\n\nH276 is unresolved and closed. The interaction of TP53 loss and high SLFN11 expression on dependency_USP7 gives -0.0322 (-0.2021 to 0.1376) with direction +1. The point estimate is opposite the registered support direction, but the interval is wide and includes positive values. There is no evidence for a clinically meaningful modification of the TP53-loss/USP7 association by SLFN11-high status.\n\nH277 is unresolved and closed. The interaction of PTEN loss and high hypoxia on dependency_STAT3 gives -0.0413 (-0.1601 to 0.0775) with direction -1. Again, the point estimate is opposite the registered positive-interaction direction, but the interval is wide. The robust PTEN/STAT3 main effect should not be replaced by this imprecise subgroup interaction.\n\nAudit: the exposures are measured genomic, transcriptional, or technical variables, and outcomes are CRISPR dependency scores, so direct outcome leakage is unlikely. However, all clean-background and interaction analyses are selected by eligibility filters and can introduce collider-like selection. Multiplicity is high and independent validation budget is exhausted. Therefore, the new accepted claims should be interpreted as internally replicated associations in selected strata of this supplied dataset, not as causal mechanisms or externally generalizable conclusions. No validation request was made because the remaining voluntary validation budget is zero.",
