  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered direction. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed mean difference or interaction estimate supports stronger dependency in the exposed group or a stronger registered main effect in the interaction subgroup; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate supports weaker dependency or a stronger weaker-dependency effect in the named subgroup. To apply the 10% clinical-significance instruction to negative dependency scores, I used approximately 10% of the absolute population mean dependency score for the relevant outcome as a practical magnitude benchmark. This is a heuristic because group means are not displayed, but it gives roughly: SMARCA2 about 0.042, USP7 about 0.018, STAT3 about 0.028, WRN about 0.030, MDM2 about 0.027, EGFR/ERBB2/KRAS/DHX9 about 0.025, and smaller for outcomes centered closer to zero.\n\nH20 is accepted and closed. In Avana-library screens, SMARCA4 loss versus SMARCA4 intact shows a positive signed difference of +0.781 (95% Welch interval 0.760 to 0.802) for dependency_SMARCA2. This is far above the SMARCA2 benchmark and indicates markedly stronger SMARCA2 dependency in SMARCA4-loss lines. It reinforces the already robust dataset-specific SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 association within a common library context. Acceptance remains internal to this dataset and is not causal or external evidence.\n\nH279 is accepted and closed with caution. In KRAS-wild-type, BRAF-wild-type, higher-screen-quality lines excluding TP53 loss, KEAP1 loss, MSI-high, SMARCA4 loss, and PTEN loss, NF1 loss versus NF1 intact shows a positive signed difference of +0.1118 (0.0357 to 0.1880) for dependency_KRAS with direction +1. This indicates weaker KRAS dependency among NF1-loss lines, far above the KRAS benchmark, and the interval excludes zero and the benchmark. The exposed cell is only 27, and the analysis is highly selected, so the claim is accepted only as a restricted dataset-specific association, not as a general NF1-loss effect or mechanism.\n\nH281 is accepted and closed. In higher-screen-quality, higher-Cas9 lines without KRAS, BRAF, EGFR, or ERBB2 alterations, PTEN loss versus PTEN intact shows a positive signed difference of +0.2358 (0.0868 to 0.3847) for dependency_STAT3 with direction -1. This indicates substantially stronger STAT3 dependency among PTEN-loss lines in an oncogene-wild-type setting. The point estimate and lower bound are well above the STAT3 benchmark, despite exposed n=31. This supports robustness of the PTEN-loss/STAT3 association beyond common driver-altered backgrounds, but remains a dataset-specific association.\n\nH50 is accepted and closed. In the adenocarcinoma/non-Avana subgroup, TP53 loss versus intact TP53 gives +0.3342 (0.0521 to 0.6164) for dependency_USP7 with direction +1. The interval is wide because exposed n=32 and comparator n=133, but the lower bound is above the USP7 benchmark and supports weaker USP7 dependency with TP53 loss. This is consistent with the broader internally supported TP53-loss/USP7 pattern. It is accepted only as an internal association in a small selected context.\n\nH38 is rejected and closed. ARID1A loss overall versus ARID1A intact shows a negative signed estimate of -0.0168 (-0.0566 to 0.0229) for dependency_SMARCA2. The interval includes zero and the positive upper bound is below the SMARCA2 benchmark, so there is no evidence for a clinically meaningful stronger SMARCA2 dependency with ARID1A loss. The negative point estimate is not strong enough to establish the opposite effect; the registered direction is not supported.\n\nH39 is rejected and closed. Overall NF1 loss versus intact NF1 gives a negative signed estimate of -0.0202 (-0.0491 to 0.0086) for dependency_KRAS with direction -1. This does not support stronger KRAS dependency with NF1 loss; the point estimate instead reflects the opposite direction, but the interval crosses zero and is not sufficiently precise to establish a clinically meaningful opposite effect. It is closed because it is inconsistent with the restricted KRAS-wild-type accepted context.\n\nH36 is unresolved and closed because the non-Avana MSI-high versus MSS comparison has only 10 exposed observations, below the minimum-cell-size requirement, so it cannot be interpreted.\n\nH278 is unresolved and closed. RB1 loss in a clean high-quality background gives +0.0396 (-0.0188 to 0.0980) for dependency_DHX9. The point estimate is near or above the DHX9 benchmark and suggests weaker DHX9 dependency, but the interval includes zero and is too imprecise given exposed n=45 and selection. No clinically meaningful dataset-specific association is accepted.\n\nH280 is unresolved and closed. KRAS mutation in a restricted high-quality canonical-background set gives +0.0289 (-0.0110 to 0.0687) for dependency_KRAS. The point estimate is slightly above the KRAS benchmark but the interval includes zero and the result remains too uncertain to accept a stronger KRAS-dependency association, consistent with the inconsistent KRAS-mutation evidence overall.\n\nH282 is unresolved and closed. The MTAP-loss interaction probe for CDKN2A-loss effects on MDM2 dependency within TP53-intact, higher-quality lines gives +0.0559 (-0.0899 to 0.2018). The point estimate exceeds the MDM2 benchmark, but the interval is very wide and crosses zero. This cannot establish clinically meaningful modification of the CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 association by MTAP co-loss.\n\nH283 is rejected and closed. The high-aneuploidy interaction probe for SMARCA4-loss effects on SMARCA2 dependency gives -0.0257 (-0.0862 to 0.0348). The negative signed estimate is opposite the registered stronger-effect direction, and the positive upper bound is below the SMARCA2 benchmark. The evidence does not support clinically meaningful strengthening of the SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 association in high-aneuploidy lines.\n\nH284 is unresolved and closed. In a cleaner high-quality background excluding KRAS mutation, ERBB2 amplification, TP53 loss, KEAP1 loss, and MSI-high, EGFR amplification gives +0.0130 (-0.0491 to 0.0751) for dependency_EGFR. The point estimate is below the EGFR benchmark and the interval is wide. This remains unresolved but should not be further expanded because EGFR amplification has produced borderline or null evidence across multiple selected contexts and multiplicity is high.\n\nAudit: direct outcome leakage is unlikely, but these are observational cell-line associations. The highly filtered comparisons are vulnerable to selection and collider-like artifacts, especially H279, H281, and H50 with small exposed cells. Multiplicity is substantial, and independent validation budget is exhausted. Therefore, accepted claims are dataset-specific associations in defined eligibility or subgroup settings only; they are not causal, therapeutic, or generalizable claims. No validation was requested because no new validation capacity remains.",
