  "narrative": "No assessments are due and no independent-validation budget remains, so this final exploration phase registers targeted internal specificity, pathway-alternative, modifier, and robustness tests rather than attempting further validation. All estimates will be interpreted as signed to the registered direction. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed mean difference indicates raw more-negative values in the exposed group, i.e. stronger dependency; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate indicates raw higher/less-negative values, i.e. weaker dependency. For interactions, the raw contrast is the named-subgroup contrast minus the same-population complement contrast, and it is then signed to the registered interaction direction.\n\nThe EGFR amplification association remains borderline rather than accepted. I therefore registered an interaction by KRAS-mutation status. Because EGFR dependence may be strongest in an EGFR-amplified, KRAS-wild-type background and weaker when KRAS mutation provides parallel RAS-pathway activation, the named subgroup is KRAS-wild-type and direction is -1. A positive signed estimate would mean the EGFR-amplified versus EGFR-negative contrast is more negative in KRAS-wild-type lines than in KRAS-mutant lines. This is a specificity test for the unresolved EGFR amplification signal, not a new overall main-effect claim.\n\nTwo RAS-pathway probes address underexamined dependency_RIT1 associations. First, I test whether KRAS mutation is associated with stronger RIT1 dependency in a restricted high-screen-quality, high-Cas9, relatively co-mutation-clean background. RIT1 is a RAS-family GTPase, so a KRAS-mutant effect could reflect pathway co-dependence, but earlier KRAS-mutation estimates were inconsistent; this test is falsifiable and expected to be null unless the association is concentrated in cleaner high-quality lines. Second, I test NF1 loss among KRAS-wild-type high-quality lines for RIT1 dependency. If NF1 loss activates alternative RAS signaling, it may bypass RIT1 dependency and produce a less-negative score, so direction is +1. This is an internal mechanistic alternative to the accepted restricted NF1-loss/KRAS-dependency association, not evidence of generalizability.\n\nI registered one high-quality/cleaned follow-up for MYC amplification and KIF18A dependency. MYC amplification was broadly null overall for mitotic and transcriptional outcomes, but if there is a clinically meaningful proliferative/mitotic dependency effect, it might be more visible under high screen quality and adequate Cas9 activity. The expected direction is -1 for stronger KIF18A dependency, but the prior broad null and modest effect-size benchmark make this an unresolved specificity probe.\n\nFor accepted PTEN loss and STAT3 dependency, I registered an interaction by PIK3CA mutation status to test pathway independence. PTEN loss and PIK3CA mutation both affect PI3K/AKT signaling, and prior stratified estimates suggested the PTEN effect may persist in both PIK3CA-mutant and PIK3CA-wild-type contexts. The registered direction is -1, but anticipated modification is zero: a large positive estimate would indicate stronger PTEN loss effect in PIK3CA-mutant lines, a large negative estimate stronger effect in PIK3CA-wild-type lines, and an estimate near zero would support robustness across PI3K-pathway genetic context.\n\nI registered two specificity controls for accepted associations. KEAP1 loss with WRN dependency and TP53 loss with NFE2L2 dependency in high-quality screens are negative-control probes: if the accepted KEAP1/NFE2L2 and TP53/USP7 associations are reasonably specific, these effects should be near zero or below practical magnitude. Direction and anticipated direction are registered mainly to provide signed outputs, with anticipated zero indicating no expected clinically meaningful effect.\n\nFinally, I registered WRN dependency in MSI-high versus MSS under lower screen quality as a robustness probe for the accepted MSI-high/WRN association. The main accepted association is robust in high-quality and several co-mutation-restricted contexts, but lower-quality screens could attenuate it. If the association persists even with lower screen quality, it supports internal robustness; if attenuated, the accepted high-quality finding may depend partly on screen quality. All proposed claims remain dataset-specific associations and are vulnerable to multiplicity and selection artifacts.\n\nThe practical magnitude benchmark remains approximately 10% of the absolute mean dependency score: roughly 0.025 for EGFR, KRAS, DHX9, ERBB2, POLR2A, RIT1, RPL11, TMED10, and KIF18A; 0.028 for STAT3; 0.030 for WRN; 0.018 for USP7; 0.042 for SMARCA2; and 0.009 for NFE2L2. Because validation budget is exhausted, none of these proposals is intended to convert unresolved claims into externally validated claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 24 final exploration plan. No assessments due; validation budget exhausted. Remaining analysis should prioritize specificity and unresolved mechanistic candidates rather than broad subgroup slicing. Registered: EGFR amplification interaction by KRAS wild-type status to test whether unresolved EGFR dependency is stronger in KRAS-wild-type than KRAS-mutant contexts; KRAS mutation with RIT1 dependency in high-quality/clean backgrounds; NF1 loss with RIT1 dependency among KRAS-wild-type high-quality lines; MYC amplification with KIF18A dependency in high-quality/high-Cas9 lines; PTEN loss STAT3 interaction by PIK3CA mutation to test PI3K-pathway independence; KEAP1 loss with WRN and TP53 loss with NFE2L2 as specificity controls; WRN/MSI association in lower-screen-quality lines for robustness. Interpret all results as dataset-specific associations only. Accepted main associations remain controller-maintained; these new claims can only refine or constrain interpretation, not establish causal/external claims."
