  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 mean differences, a positive signed estimate supports stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1 mean differences, a positive signed estimate supports weaker dependency in the exposed group. For interactions, a positive signed estimate supports a stronger effect in the named subgroup relative to its complement, in the registered direction. I used approximately 10% of the absolute population mean dependency score as a practical benchmark, yielding roughly 0.025 for EGFR/RIT1/KIF18A, 0.028 for STAT3, 0.030 for WRN, 0.009 for NFE2L2, 0.042 for SMARCA2, and 0.018 for USP7. These are dataset-specific associations only; no causal, therapeutic, or external generalization is inferred. No validation was requested because the new validation budget is zero.\n\nH285 is rejected and closed. The EGFR-amplification-by-KRAS-wild-type interaction for dependency_EGFR gives a negative signed estimate of -0.0372 (95% CI -0.0949 to 0.0205), opposite the registered stronger-effect direction. The upper bound is below the EGFR practical benchmark of about 0.025, so there is no evidence for a clinically meaningful interaction in the registered direction. Because the interval still includes zero and the reversed interaction was not pre-specified as a new claim, I close this branch rather than propose a reversal.\n\nH286 is unresolved and closed. KRAS mutation versus KRAS wild type in high-screen-quality, high-Cas9, relatively clean co-mutation backgrounds gives a positive signed estimate of +0.0391 for dependency_RIT1, suggesting stronger RIT1 dependency among KRAS-mutant lines, but the interval (-0.0074 to 0.0855) crosses zero and exposed/comparator cells are modest. It is too uncertain to accept despite exceeding the RIT1 benchmark in the point estimate. This is consistent with the broader inconsistency of KRAS-mutation predictors and should not be expanded further.\n\nH287 is unresolved and closed. NF1 loss among KRAS-wild-type, high-screen-quality lines gives +0.0183 for dependency_RIT1, nominally in the predicted weaker-dependency direction, but the interval (-0.0283 to 0.0650) includes zero and the point estimate is below the RIT1 practical benchmark. This does not support a clinically meaningful RIT1 modification by NF1 loss in this context.\n\nH288 is unresolved and closed. MYC amplification in high-screen-quality, high-Cas9 backgrounds gives +0.0302 for dependency_KIF18A, above the KIF18A benchmark, but the interval (-0.0135 to 0.0740) includes zero and is imprecise. Given the prior broad null for MYC amplification across mitotic and transcriptional outcomes, this is a underpowered specificity probe, not a new accepted association.\n\nH289 is unresolved and closed. The PIK3CA-mutation interaction probe for PTEN-loss effects on dependency_STAT3 gives an estimate very close to zero, +0.0013 (95% CI -0.1499 to 0.1524). The point estimate suggests no meaningful modification of the accepted PTEN-loss/STAT3 association, but the interval is too wide to rule out a large interaction in either direction. This neither supports nor refutes a stable PI3K-pathway modifier; it leaves modifier uncertainty unresolved.\n\nH290 is rejected and closed as a specificity control. KEAP1 loss versus KEAP1 intact in high-screen-quality screens gives -0.0206 for dependency_WRN, opposite the registered weaker-dependency direction, with the positive upper bound (0.0201) below the WRN benchmark. There is no clinically meaningful evidence that KEAP1 loss produces a WRN-dependency signal analogous to the accepted KEAP1/NFE2L2 association.\n\nH291 is unresolved and closed. TP53 loss versus intact TP53 in high-screen-quality screens gives +0.0159 for dependency_NFE2L2, nominally above the small NFE2L2 benchmark but with an interval (-0.0367 to 0.0685) that includes zero. This negative-control probe is too imprecise to accept a clinically meaningful TP53-loss/NFE2L2-dependency association, although it does not strongly rule one out either.\n\nH292 is accepted and closed. MSI-high versus non-MSI-high lines under lower screen quality show a very large positive signed difference for dependency_WRN, +0.7813 (95% CI 0.7303 to 0.8323), far above the WRN benchmark and excluding zero. This provides strong internal robustness evidence that the MSI-high/WRN association is not confined only to higher-screen-quality subsets. It remains a dataset-specific association, not evidence of external biological mechanism or clinical utility.\n\nH104-H106 assess constructed research markers D, E, and F as negative-control probes. H104 is rejected and closed: research marker D gives +0.0024 for dependency_SMARCA2 (95% CI -0.0298 to 0.0345), and the upper bound is below the SMARCA2 benchmark. H105 is rejected and closed: research marker E gives -0.0126 for dependency_WRN (95% CI -0.0356 to 0.0105), and the positive upper bound is below the WRN benchmark. H106 is unresolved and closed: research marker F gives +0.0194 for dependency_USP7 (95% CI -0.0153 to 0.0541), crossing zero despite a point estimate slightly above the USP7 benchmark. Overall, these constructed markers do not provide supported predictor signals, supporting specificity for the accepted genetic associations, with the minor caveat that marker F remains imprecise.\n\nOverall, this batch leaves one clear new accepted robustness result (MSI-high/WRN in lower screen quality) and otherwise closes specificity, modifier, negative-control, and marker probes as unsupported or unresolved. Multiplicity and selection are major caveats, especially for highly filtered KRAS and PTEN context analyses. No new validation request is possible or warranted.",
  "research_notes": "Final appraise state: all due claims assessed; validation budget exhausted, so no validation request. H292 accepts MSI-high/WRN robustness even under lower screen-quality eligibility, strengthening the already accepted MSI-high/WRN dataset-specific association. H285 rejects the EGFR-amplification interaction in the registered KRAS-wild-type direction; EGFR amplification remains unresolved overall. H286-H288 remain unresolved but closed: KRAS-mutation/RIT1, NF1-loss/RIT1, and MYC-amplification/KIF18A probes are underpowered and not accepted. H289 remains unresolved: PTEN-loss/STAT3 interaction by PIK3CA mutation is near zero but too imprecise to rule out modification; accepted PTEN/STAT3 main effect should remain interpreted without a stable PI3K-pathway modifier. H290 rejects KEAP1-loss/WRN cross-pathway control; H291 leaves TP53-loss/NFE2L2 negative-control unresolved but unsupported. Research-marker controls: H104 marker D/SMARCA2 rejected, H105 marker E/WRN rejected, H106 marker F/USP7 unresolved but unsupported. Main supported dataset-specific associations remain: SMARCA4-loss/strong SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1-loss/weak NFE2L2 dependency; TP53-loss/weak USP7 dependency; MSI-high/strong WRN dependency; PTEN-loss/strong STAT3 dependency; CDKN2A-loss/strong MDM2 dependency; restricted NF1-loss/weak KRAS dependency in KRAS-wild-type high-quality contexts. Key negative or unsupported predictors include broad KRAS-mutation/KRAS dependency, BRAF V600E/BRAF dependency, EGFR amplification/EGFR dependency, ERBB2 amplification/ERBB2 dependency, TP53-loss/MDM2 dependency, MYC amplification across DHX9/RPL11/POLR2A/KIF18A, PIK3CA mutation for STAT3/EGFR/ERBB2 dependency, and constructed markers as supported predictors. Final stance: associations are internal to the supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable."
