  "narrative": "No response deadlines were listed, no new validation estimates were delivered during this synthesis step, and the independent-validation budget is exhausted. I therefore made no validation requests and made no status changes. The final synthesis is based on the full discovery/validation ledger and should be read as internal associations within the supplied lung-lineage CRISPR dataset, not as causal effects, therapeutic vulnerabilities, or generalizable biological claims. Where independent validation exists, it supports internal reproducibility of the stated direction and magnitude, not external validity.\\n\\nAll returned estimates are already signed to the hypothesis direction. For direction -1 mean differences, a positive signed estimate indicates a raw more-negative dependency score in the exposed group, i.e. stronger dependency. For direction +1 mean differences, a positive signed estimate indicates a raw less-negative dependency score in the exposed group, i.e. weaker dependency. I continued to use roughly 10% of the absolute mean dependency score as a practical benchmark, approximately 0.042 for SMARCA2, 0.030 for WRN, 0.028 for STAT3, 0.025 for KRAS/EGFR/ERBB2/DHX9/POLR2A/RPL11/RIT1/KIF18A, 0.018 for USP7, and 0.009 for NFE2L2. Acceptance required a meaningful point estimate, a 95% interval excluding zero, cell-size validity, and enough consistency to survive multiplicity and selection concerns.\\n\\nSUPPORTED DATASET-SPECIFIC ASSOCIATIONS:\\n1. SMARCA4 loss is strongly associated with SMARCA2 dependency. The contrast is approximately 0.75 to 0.82 dependency-score units more negative, far above the SMARCA2 benchmark. This is the most robust accepted association and is supported across library, screen-quality, Cas9-activity, lineage, co-alteration, growth-rate, expression, ploidy, and aneuploidy strata; independent validation in a higher screen-quality stratum gives about 0.81, with the interval still excluding zero (R31/R40). Interpretation: internal co-dependency signal, not validated clinical synthetic lethality outside this dataset.\\n2. KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency. The accepted direction is the reverse of the original stronger-dependency hypothesis: H22 accepts a positive signed contrast of about 0.78 to 0.81, corresponding to raw less-negative scores, i.e. reduced dependency (R6/R26). This result is independently validated and is robust across lineage, screen-quality, Cas9-activity, library, co-alteration, growth-rate, EMT, and clean-background contexts. The original H6 direction is rejected because it asks for a stronger-dependency effect that the data do not support.\\n3. TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency. The accepted signed contrast is roughly 0.27 to 0.49, corresponding to raw less-negative USP7 scores (R8/R39). It is robust across library, screen quality, Cas9 activity, lineage, ploidy, growth rate, co-mutation, EMT, AXL expression, MYC expression, interferon, and other strata, with several independent validations. A small negative control using TP53 loss and NFE2L2 was unresolved but does not support an analogous cross-pathway signal.\\n4. MSI-high status is associated with markedly stronger WRN dependency. The accepted signed contrast is approximately 0.77 to 0.83, far above the WRN benchmark (R9/R53). The association replicates in adenocarcinoma, higher screen quality, high Cas9 activity, mutation-burden, ploidy, interferon, proliferation, lower screen quality, and co-mutation-restricted analyses, including H292 in lower-quality screens (R296). It is independently validated. This is a very strong internal association, but the evidence does not show external biological mechanism or clinical utility.\\n5. PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency. Accepted contrasts are roughly 0.19 to 0.51 signed units, corresponding to raw more-negative STAT3 scores. Several representative contexts are independently validated, including overall high-quality screens, low Cas9 activity, TP53-loss, PIK3CA-mutant, PIK3CA-wild-type, high IFN-gamma, EMT, stemness, proliferation, and clean high-quality contexts (for example R80/R66, R109/R107, R113/R106, R123/R120, R132/R133). Modifier probes did not establish a stable interaction, so PTEN loss should be interpreted as the robust predictor in this dataset rather than a context that necessarily modifies or is modified by other accepted markers.\\n6. CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. Accepted signed contrasts are approximately 0.10 to 0.16, corresponding to raw more-negative MDM2 scores (R168/R177/R183/R178/R185/R209/R219/R234/R270). This is consistent across TP53-intact and TP53-loss contexts, ploidy strata, mutation burden, growth rate, Cas9 activity, screen quality, MTAP stratification, and relatively clean co-mutation backgrounds. It was not independently validated, but it is internally consistent and far below the null benchmark. It should be described as an association, not a causal mechanism.\\n7. NF1 loss is associated with weaker KRAS dependency in restricted KRAS-wild-type, higher-screen-quality settings. The accepted signed contrasts are about 0.06 to 0.11 for KRAS-wild-type high-quality and highly restricted backgrounds (R246/R278), corresponding to raw less-negative KRAS scores. This finding should be narrowly interpreted because of eligibility selection, small exposed cells, and the broader inconsistency of KRAS-pathway predictors. It is not validated and should not be generalized beyond the selected KRAS-wild-type context.\\n\\nNEGATIVE OR UNSUPPORTED FINDINGS:\\nThe canonical alteration-own-dependency hypotheses were not supported as robust associations. Broad KRAS mutation versus wild type for KRAS dependency remained unresolved/inconsistent (R1/R262/R264/R279), and BRAF V600E was not supported for BRAF dependency (R2/R104). EGFR amplification remains unresolved rather than accepted: the main discovery estimate is borderline and validation is too wide to confirm (R3/R13), while cleaner or background-stratified probes are null or imprecise. ERBB2 amplification for ERBB2 dependency is near zero and not meaningful (R4). TP53 loss was not robustly associated with stronger MDM2 dependency (R7/R26/R50/R49/R78), in contrast to the supported CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 association. MYC amplification showed no meaningful association with DHX9, RPL11, POLR2A, or KIF18A, including in high MYC expression or higher-screen-quality contexts (R10-R12/R14/R119/R124/R132/R292). PIK3CA mutation was not accepted as a meaningful predictor of STAT3, EGFR, or ERBB2 dependency (R63/R130/R242/R243). Constructed research markers D, E, and F did not provide supported STAT3 or cross-outcome predictors; marker D and E were rejected against their probes, and marker F remained imprecise and unsupported (R88-R90/R297-R299). Most interaction probes for lineage, library, Cas9 activity, growth rate, aneuploidy, IFN-gamma, EMT, AXL, serum, MTAP, PIK3CA, and co-alteration contexts did not establish clinically meaningful modification. In particular, EGFR-amplification interaction by KRAS status rejected the registered interaction (R289), SMARCA4/SMARCA2 interactions were null, TP53/USP7 interactions were unsupported, and the PTEN/STAT3 PIK3CA interaction was near zero but too imprecise to establish or exclude modification.\\n\\nUNRESOLVED UNCERTAINTY:\\nEGFR amplification remains borderline: discovery and validation intervals are too wide or close to zero to accept, and specificity probes did not identify a clean context that rescues the signal. KRAS mutation effects remain inconsistent; a high-Cas9 KRAS-mutant/KRAS-dependency estimate is directionally meaningful but not accepted because of prior broad nulls and selection concerns. NF1 loss effects outside selected KRAS-wild-type high-quality contexts remain unresolved. RB1-loss/DHX9 dependency, broader TP53-loss/NFE2L2 cross-pathway controls, and some high-IFN or expression-stratified signals remain imprecise or too underpowered. MTAP modification of CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 dependency remains unresolved because interaction intervals are wide. Modifier interactions for PTEN/STAT3 and TP53/USP7 remain unresolved or unsupported. Finally, the accepted context-specific associations remain vulnerable to multiplicity, collider-like selection, and residual confounding by screen or genomic features, even after many stratified checks.\\n\\nFINAL SCIENTIFIC STANCE:\\nThe dataset provides strong internal evidence for associations involving SMARCA4 loss/SMARCA2, KEAP1 loss/NFE2L2 reverse direction, TP53 loss/USP7 reverse direction, MSI-high/WRN, PTEN loss/STAT3, CDKN2A loss/MDM2, and a narrow NF1-loss/KRAS-wild-type association. These are the main supported findings. Canonical mutation- or amplification-own-dependency relationships are not robustly supported in this dataset. No claim should be stated as causal, therapeutic, or generalizable without independent external replication.\\n\\nMOST INFORMATIVE NEXT ANALYSIS:\\nThe highest-value follow-up is external or truly independent replication of the six main accepted associations and the narrow NF1-loss association, with pre-specified models adjusting for library, screen quality, Cas9 activity, lineage, ploidy, mutation burden, proliferation, and co-occurring SMARCA4/KEAP1/TP53/MSI/PTEN/CDKN2A/MTAP/NF1/KRAS/EGFR/ERBB2 alterations. If only internal work is possible, further analysis should be pre-specified on unresolved candidates only, with adequate cell sizes, multiplicity control, and no additional broad subgroup slicing.",
