  "narrative": "All estimates are interpreted as already signed to the registered hypothesis direction: for direction -1 mean differences, positive values indicate a raw more-negative dependency score in the exposed group, i.e. stronger dependency; for direction +1 mean differences, positive values indicate a raw less-negative score, i.e. weaker dependency. For interactions, positive values indicate a stronger registered effect in the named subgroup relative to its complement. I used roughly 10% of the absolute mean dependency score as a practical benchmark, approximately 0.028 for STAT3, 0.027 for MDM2, 0.009 for NFE2L2, 0.018 for USP7, 0.030 for WRN, 0.025 for KRAS, and 0.042 for SMARCA2. No validation was requested because the remaining new-validation budget is zero.\n\nH293 is unresolved and closed. KEAP1 loss versus KEAP1 intact in high-screen-quality, adequate-Cas9 screens gives a near-zero signed STAT3 contrast, +0.0006 (95% CI -0.0418 to +0.0430). The point estimate is far below the STAT3 benchmark, but the interval still admits clinically meaningful values in both directions. This does not support a meaningful KEAP1-to-STAT3 association, but it is not precise enough to reject the possibility of a moderate modifier effect.\n\nH294 is accepted and closed. PTEN loss versus PTEN intact among lower-screen-quality lines gives +0.300 (95% CI 0.216 to 0.383) for dependency_STAT3, far above the benchmark and excluding zero. This strongly supports the dataset-specific PTEN-loss/STAT3 association in a lower screen-quality subset. It remains an internal association, not evidence of causality or external generalizability.\n\nH295 is accepted and closed. CDKN2A loss versus intact CDKN2A among lower-screen-quality lines gives +0.086 (95% CI 0.037 to 0.135) for dependency_MDM2, above the MDM2 benchmark and excluding zero. This supports the robustness of the CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 association when screen quality is lower. Because many related tests were performed, interpretation should remain conservative and association-specific.\n\nH296 is accepted and closed. KEAP1 loss versus KEAP1 intact in lower-screen-quality lines gives +0.805 (95% CI 0.774 to 0.836) for dependency_NFE2L2, a very large effect far above the NFE2L2 benchmark. This robustly supports the accepted reverse-direction association: KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency in this dataset, not stronger dependency.\n\nH297 is accepted and closed. TP53 loss versus intact TP53 in lower-screen-quality lines gives +0.341 (95% CI 0.219 to 0.463) for dependency_USP7, far above the USP7 benchmark. This supports the accepted reverse-direction association: TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency within this dataset.\n\nH298 is accepted and closed, with cell-size caution. MSI-high versus non-MSI-high lines with lower Cas9 activity gives +0.798 (95% CI 0.732 to 0.864) for dependency_WRN. The exposed cell has only 27 observations, but it meets the minimum cell-size rule and the effect is extremely large and excludes zero. The MSI-high/WRN association therefore remains very strong under lower editing efficiency, though small exposed counts warrant caution.\n\nH299 is unresolved and closed. NF1 loss versus NF1 intact among KRAS-wild-type, lower-screen-quality lines gives +0.012 (95% CI -0.043 to +0.066) for dependency_KRAS. The point estimate is below the KRAS benchmark and the interval includes both no effect and clinically meaningful effects. This does not support an accepted lower-quality KRAS-wild-type NF1 association.\n\nH300 is rejected and closed. MTAP loss versus MTAP intact among CDKN2A-intact lines gives +0.004 (95% CI -0.018 to +0.026) for dependency_MDM2. The positive interval upper bound is below the MDM2 practical benchmark, so there is no evidence that MTAP loss acts as a meaningful independent modifier in this context. The estimate is essentially null.\n\nH301 is unresolved and closed. The PTEN-loss interaction probe for CDKN2A-loss effects on dependency_MDM2 in high-quality screens gives +0.079 (95% CI -0.079 to +0.237). The point estimate is above benchmark, but the interval is too wide and crosses zero, and the exposed subgroup cell is small. The data cannot establish or exclude a clinically meaningful PTEN-dependent modification of the CDKN2A/MDM2 association.\n\nH302 is rejected and closed. PIK3CA mutation versus wild type in high-quality, TP53-intact lines gives a negative signed MDM2 contrast, -0.025 (95% CI -0.065 to +0.015). The effect points opposite the registered stronger-dependency direction, and the upper bound is below the MDM2 benchmark. This provides no evidence for a meaningful PI3K-pathway association with MDM2 dependency in this setting.\n\nH303 is unresolved and closed. PTEN loss versus intact in high-quality, TP53-intact lines gives a negative signed MDM2 contrast, -0.010 (95% CI -0.055 to +0.036). The point estimate is not meaningful, but the interval includes clinically meaningful positive values and zero, so a modest PTEN-to-MDM2 effect cannot be ruled out despite the negative point estimate.\n\nH304 is accepted and closed. SMARCA4 loss versus intact among adherent cultures gives +0.788 (95% CI 0.765 to 0.811) for dependency_SMARCA2, far above the SMARCA2 benchmark. This supports the core SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 dependency association in the common adherent-culture subset. It remains a dataset-specific association and not a claim about causal synthetic lethality or clinical generalizability.\n\nOverall, the low-screen-quality/low-Cas9 robustness checks support persistence of the major accepted associations for PTEN/STAT3, CDKN2A/MDM2, KEAP1/NFE2L2 reverse direction, TP53/USP7 reverse direction, MSI-high/WRN, and SMARCA4/SMARCA2. The remaining unresolved probes are limited by imprecision rather than by contradictory evidence. Multiplicity and residual confounding remain important caveats, especially for highly filtered or small-cell strata.",
